1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,716
Professor: The bystander effect is a social psychological theory according to

3
00:00:06,716 --> 00:00:10,400
which individuals are less likely to offer help to a victim

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,120
when other people are present.

5
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:18,040
The theory was first proposed by researchers in the 1960s, based on observations.

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:22,553
The bystander effect can be explained through two primary factors:

7
00:00:22,553 --> 00:00:25,260
diffusion of responsibility and social influence.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:30,980
Diffusion of responsibility occurs when people believe that someone else will take action,

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,060
and so they feel less personal responsibility.

10
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:41,210
Social influence involves looking to others for cues on how to behave, especially in ambiguous situations.

11
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:43,691
If others are not helping,

12
00:00:43,691 --> 00:00:49,150
individuals may interpret the situation as non-emergency and refrain from intervening.

13
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:57,180
More recent studies, however, have disputed the claim that the presence of more bystanders can decrease the likelihood of assistance.

14
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:03,918
One study from 2019 that analyzed video footage from multiple cities found that in 9 out of 10 incidents,

15
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:07,110
at least one bystander did take action to help.

16
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:11,300
This study found that a bystander was actually more likely to intervene

17
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,180
when more bystanders were present.

18
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:17,813
Some factors that may influence bystander behavior include the level of danger

19
00:01:17,813 --> 00:01:20,130
and whether bystanders know each other.
